1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,860
6:10 in the morning.

2
00:00:01,940 --> 00:00:03,180
You pull your bus into the

3
00:00:03,180 --> 00:00:04,100
terminal.

4
00:00:04,140 --> 00:00:05,520
A crow lands on your side

5
00:00:05,520 --> 00:00:06,320
mirror.

6
00:00:06,320 --> 00:00:07,940
It tilts its head.

7
00:00:07,940 --> 00:00:09,160
It looks at the timetable on

8
00:00:09,160 --> 00:00:10,420
your window then.

9
00:00:10,420 --> 00:00:11,400
It speaks.

10
00:00:11,680 --> 00:00:12,960
You're late, Lena.

11
00:00:13,040 --> 00:00:13,980
Four minutes.

12
00:00:14,120 --> 00:00:15,100
You blink.

13
00:00:15,220 --> 00:00:16,860
The crow blinks back.

14
00:00:16,960 --> 00:00:18,720
Behind it, three more crows read

15
00:00:18,720 --> 00:00:19,840
the route map.

16
00:00:19,860 --> 00:00:21,380
One points at Elm Street with

17
00:00:21,380 --> 00:00:22,380
its beak.

18
00:00:22,380 --> 00:00:23,540
Another one is eating your toast.

19
00:00:23,540 --> 00:00:27,000
Yesterday, crows were clever birds.

20
00:00:27,040 --> 00:00:27,840
Today,

21
00:00:27,840 --> 00:00:29,400
they think like you.

22
00:00:29,440 --> 00:00:31,900
A crow weighs about 450 grams.

23
00:00:32,060 --> 00:00:33,220
Its brain is the size of

24
00:00:33,220 --> 00:00:34,180
a walnut.

25
00:00:34,240 --> 00:00:35,180
Yours?

26
00:00:35,280 --> 00:00:37,280
About 1.4 kilograms.

27
00:00:37,420 --> 00:00:40,380
86 billion neurons overnight.

28
00:00:40,540 --> 00:00:42,060
The gap closes.

29
00:00:42,080 --> 00:00:43,840
But only inside their heads.

30
00:00:43,900 --> 00:00:45,500
Their bodies stay the same.

31
00:00:45,500 --> 00:00:46,580
No hands.

32
00:00:46,580 --> 00:00:48,340
Just a beak, two feet and

33
00:00:48,340 --> 00:00:49,460
two wings.

34
00:00:49,460 --> 00:00:50,840
That's the rule.

35
00:00:50,840 --> 00:00:52,740
So what if crows had human

36
00:00:52,740 --> 00:00:54,300
intelligence tomorrow?

37
00:00:54,500 --> 00:00:55,860
7 in the morning?

38
00:00:56,020 --> 00:00:57,040
Garbage day.

39
00:00:57,040 --> 00:00:59,160
Crows always learn trash schedules.

40
00:00:59,340 --> 00:01:01,980
Today, they read your city's calendar.

41
00:01:02,060 --> 00:01:03,800
Your bins stay shut.

42
00:01:03,840 --> 00:01:04,740
Instead,

43
00:01:04,740 --> 00:01:07,140
the crows file a complaint about

44
00:01:07,140 --> 00:01:08,360
the lids.

45
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:09,620
8 o'clock.

46
00:01:09,620 --> 00:01:11,140
Crows could always copy a few

47
00:01:11,140 --> 00:01:12,380
human words.

48
00:01:12,380 --> 00:01:14,700
Now they use full sentences.

49
00:01:14,800 --> 00:01:16,180
The one on your cafe roof

50
00:01:16,180 --> 00:01:18,160
orders your croissant.

51
00:01:18,160 --> 00:01:19,260
Politely.

52
00:01:19,260 --> 00:01:21,080
Then it asks for a receipt.

53
00:01:21,200 --> 00:01:22,480
9 o'clock.

54
00:01:22,560 --> 00:01:24,260
Some crows already bend twigs into

55
00:01:24,260 --> 00:01:25,180
hooks.

56
00:01:25,260 --> 00:01:26,500
Wild ones.

57
00:01:26,520 --> 00:01:27,520
For food.

58
00:01:27,540 --> 00:01:28,620
This morning,

59
00:01:28,620 --> 00:01:29,900
a crow bends a paper clip

60
00:01:29,900 --> 00:01:32,160
and opens the park shed.

61
00:01:32,160 --> 00:01:32,940
Why?

62
00:01:32,940 --> 00:01:34,480
It wanted the bird seed.

63
00:01:34,480 --> 00:01:35,240
Fair.

64
00:01:35,280 --> 00:01:36,180
Noon.

65
00:01:36,180 --> 00:01:37,880
Crows once drop stones into water

66
00:01:37,880 --> 00:01:39,100
to raise the level and reach

67
00:01:39,100 --> 00:01:40,060
a treat.

68
00:01:40,100 --> 00:01:40,960
Today,

69
00:01:40,960 --> 00:01:42,540
one drops pebbles into your tall

70
00:01:42,540 --> 00:01:43,760
cup of soda.

71
00:01:43,900 --> 00:01:45,680
The last fry floats up.

72
00:01:45,680 --> 00:01:46,520
Applause.

73
00:01:46,560 --> 00:01:47,280
Dev.

74
00:01:47,280 --> 00:01:49,200
Your dispatcher rolls his eyes.

75
00:01:49,280 --> 00:01:50,700
They're just birds, Lena.

76
00:01:50,740 --> 00:01:51,720
Are they?

77
00:01:51,720 --> 00:01:52,900
1 o'clock.

78
00:01:52,920 --> 00:01:55,360
In 2014, researchers watched one crow

79
00:01:55,360 --> 00:01:58,000
solve a puzzle 8 steps long.

80
00:01:58,000 --> 00:01:59,120
8 steps.

81
00:01:59,260 --> 00:02:00,160
Today,

82
00:02:00,240 --> 00:02:02,500
a crow solves the subway map.

83
00:02:02,500 --> 00:02:03,560
Before,

84
00:02:03,600 --> 00:02:04,040
you do.

85
00:02:04,040 --> 00:02:05,780
3 o'clock.

86
00:02:05,780 --> 00:02:08,000
The crows count every bus.

87
00:02:08,020 --> 00:02:09,180
Every delay.

88
00:02:09,180 --> 00:02:10,260
Your route.

89
00:02:10,260 --> 00:02:12,720
Late 11 times this month.

90
00:02:12,720 --> 00:02:14,060
They know, of course.

91
00:02:14,120 --> 00:02:14,660
They know.

92
00:02:14,660 --> 00:02:16,060
5 o'clock.

93
00:02:16,140 --> 00:02:17,360
A crow reads the news over

94
00:02:17,360 --> 00:02:18,260
your shoulder.

95
00:02:18,260 --> 00:02:20,200
Then it sighs loudly.

96
00:02:20,380 --> 00:02:21,280
6 o'clock.

97
00:02:21,280 --> 00:02:22,340
Rush hour.

98
00:02:22,380 --> 00:02:23,620
A crow on a traffic light

99
00:02:23,620 --> 00:02:25,280
shouts the next green.

100
00:02:25,320 --> 00:02:26,600
Nobody honks.

101
00:02:26,600 --> 00:02:28,360
For once sunset, winter roosts can

102
00:02:28,360 --> 00:02:30,040
hold thousands of crows.

103
00:02:30,080 --> 00:02:31,760
Tonight, the roost in the park

104
00:02:31,760 --> 00:02:33,580
becomes something new.

105
00:02:33,580 --> 00:02:35,540
A town hall and around the

106
00:02:35,540 --> 00:02:36,440
world.

107
00:02:38,880 --> 00:02:40,120
Every city.

108
00:02:40,120 --> 00:02:41,440
Every rooftop.

109
00:02:41,600 --> 00:02:42,900
Every wire.

110
00:02:42,900 --> 00:02:44,420
Then you notice something.

111
00:02:44,440 --> 00:02:46,840
The crows are watching faces.

112
00:02:47,060 --> 00:02:48,380
Your face too.

113
00:02:48,400 --> 00:02:50,160
Researchers in Seattle once wore a

114
00:02:50,160 --> 00:02:51,980
mask while catching crows.

115
00:02:52,040 --> 00:02:54,220
Years later, crows still scolded that

116
00:02:54,220 --> 00:02:55,160
mask.

117
00:02:55,160 --> 00:02:57,260
Even crows that were never there.

118
00:02:57,260 --> 00:02:58,740
They learned the grudge from the

119
00:02:58,740 --> 00:02:59,580
others.

120
00:02:59,660 --> 00:03:01,700
A crow never forgets a face.

121
00:03:01,740 --> 00:03:02,700
And now it has the words

122
00:03:02,700 --> 00:03:03,680
to say

123
00:03:03,680 --> 00:03:04,620
whose.

124
00:03:04,620 --> 00:03:05,740
The man who threw a stone

125
00:03:05,740 --> 00:03:07,320
at them in 2015.

126
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:08,780
They know his name.

127
00:03:08,780 --> 00:03:10,020
The kid who chased them on

128
00:03:10,020 --> 00:03:11,040
her scooter?

129
00:03:11,080 --> 00:03:12,020
Her too.

130
00:03:12,020 --> 00:03:13,800
Is anyone anonymous now?

131
00:03:13,800 --> 00:03:15,920
No. Not one person.

132
00:03:15,920 --> 00:03:16,960
You're safe.

133
00:03:16,960 --> 00:03:18,300
But only because of one small

134
00:03:18,300 --> 00:03:19,260
habit.

135
00:03:19,260 --> 00:03:21,020
Every night, you leave one peanut

136
00:03:21,020 --> 00:03:23,000
on the bus stop rail.

137
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:24,320
You tap it twice.

138
00:03:24,400 --> 00:03:25,360
Tap.

139
00:03:25,360 --> 00:03:26,200
Tap.

140
00:03:26,280 --> 00:03:28,180
One crow always comes.

141
00:03:28,260 --> 00:03:29,040
Pip.

142
00:03:29,160 --> 00:03:30,980
A notch in his tail feather.

143
00:03:30,980 --> 00:03:32,260
Tonight he lands on the rail

144
00:03:32,260 --> 00:03:33,580
and looks up.

145
00:03:33,640 --> 00:03:34,760
You fed us before we could

146
00:03:34,760 --> 00:03:36,620
say thank you, he says.

147
00:03:36,700 --> 00:03:38,580
So thank you.

148
00:03:38,580 --> 00:03:39,260
Rosa,

149
00:03:39,260 --> 00:03:40,580
the old parkkeeper,

150
00:03:40,580 --> 00:03:42,220
laughs from her bench.

151
00:03:42,220 --> 00:03:43,960
Told you they were watching Lena?

152
00:03:43,960 --> 00:03:44,940
You smile.

153
00:03:45,080 --> 00:03:46,100
A bird.

154
00:03:46,160 --> 00:03:47,320
Just thanked you.

155
00:03:47,320 --> 00:03:48,120
What do you even say to

156
00:03:48,120 --> 00:03:50,280
that? Then Pip goes quiet.

157
00:03:50,280 --> 00:03:51,440
The big plane tree in the

158
00:03:51,440 --> 00:03:52,820
park, he says.

159
00:03:52,920 --> 00:03:53,940
A roost.

160
00:03:53,980 --> 00:03:55,720
Thousands of us sleep there.

161
00:03:55,740 --> 00:03:57,400
At dawn, a developer named Victor

162
00:03:57,400 --> 00:03:58,540
sends a crew to cut it

163
00:03:58,540 --> 00:04:00,600
down for a parking lot.

164
00:04:00,600 --> 00:04:02,340
The crows have a plan.

165
00:04:02,340 --> 00:04:03,820
Thousands of birds.

166
00:04:03,820 --> 00:04:05,420
Thousands of voices.

167
00:04:05,580 --> 00:04:06,680
Is that enough to stop a

168
00:04:06,680 --> 00:04:07,680
chainsaw?

169
00:04:07,680 --> 00:04:08,760
Do the math.

170
00:04:08,760 --> 00:04:10,620
450 grams each.

171
00:04:10,740 --> 00:04:11,880
No hands.

172
00:04:11,880 --> 00:04:13,000
They can shout.

173
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:14,120
They can circle.

174
00:04:14,120 --> 00:04:15,680
They can't pull a cable.

175
00:04:15,680 --> 00:04:17,180
They can't sign a form.

176
00:04:17,180 --> 00:04:18,420
They can't stand in front of

177
00:04:18,420 --> 00:04:19,340
a truck.

178
00:04:19,340 --> 00:04:20,440
Not really.

179
00:04:20,440 --> 00:04:21,800
They need you.

180
00:04:21,800 --> 00:04:22,820
5:30.

181
00:04:22,820 --> 00:04:24,180
The crew arrives.

182
00:04:24,220 --> 00:04:25,640
Engines rumble.

183
00:04:25,640 --> 00:04:27,440
Your shift starts at 6.

184
00:04:27,500 --> 00:04:29,180
Your phone buzzes, Dev.

185
00:04:29,180 --> 00:04:30,500
Clock in, Lena.

186
00:04:30,540 --> 00:04:31,720
Or don't come back.

187
00:04:31,720 --> 00:04:34,040
Then a sound on your window.

188
00:04:34,040 --> 00:04:35,360
Tap, tap, Pip.

189
00:04:35,500 --> 00:04:36,600
Time slows.

190
00:04:36,740 --> 00:04:38,820
11 minutes until the first cut.

191
00:04:38,820 --> 00:04:40,700
2 kilometers to the terminal.

192
00:04:40,740 --> 00:04:42,020
You look at the bus.

193
00:04:42,020 --> 00:04:43,520
You look at the tree.

194
00:04:43,540 --> 00:04:44,660
One choice.

195
00:04:44,880 --> 00:04:46,140
Keep your job.

196
00:04:46,180 --> 00:04:47,580
Or keep your word.

197
00:04:47,620 --> 00:04:49,240
You switch off the engine.

198
00:04:49,260 --> 00:04:50,740
You walk to the tree.

199
00:04:50,740 --> 00:04:51,740
You stand right in front of

200
00:04:51,740 --> 00:04:52,660
the crew.

201
00:04:52,740 --> 00:04:54,200
Victor waves a permit.

202
00:04:54,200 --> 00:04:55,760
Move, he says.

203
00:04:55,840 --> 00:04:56,900
You don't.

204
00:04:56,900 --> 00:04:58,220
Then a crow drops something at

205
00:04:58,220 --> 00:04:59,140
your feet.

206
00:04:59,140 --> 00:05:01,700
Paper, pages from Victor's truck.

207
00:05:01,780 --> 00:05:02,900
Dev comes running.

208
00:05:02,900 --> 00:05:04,020
Still angry.

209
00:05:04,080 --> 00:05:05,120
He reads them.

210
00:05:05,120 --> 00:05:06,600
Then he reads them again.

211
00:05:06,660 --> 00:05:09,080
The permit was never signed.

212
00:05:09,100 --> 00:05:10,460
The engines stop.

213
00:05:10,560 --> 00:05:11,800
The tree stays.

214
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:13,540
That night, the crows hold their

215
00:05:13,540 --> 00:05:14,940
first town hall.

216
00:05:14,940 --> 00:05:16,240
One new rule.

217
00:05:16,240 --> 00:05:18,060
Crows keep the roost.

218
00:05:18,060 --> 00:05:20,000
People keep the sidewalks.

219
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:21,700
Dev keeps your shift.

220
00:05:21,700 --> 00:05:22,600
Barely.

221
00:05:22,600 --> 00:05:24,700
A crow never forgets a face.

222
00:05:24,740 --> 00:05:25,660
And now it has the words

223
00:05:25,660 --> 00:05:26,680
to say?

224
00:05:26,700 --> 00:05:28,460
Whose? Yours.

225
00:05:28,500 --> 00:05:29,740
They say thank you.

226
00:05:29,740 --> 00:05:31,320
So how real is this?

227
00:05:31,380 --> 00:05:33,720
Crows really bend twigs into tools.

228
00:05:33,800 --> 00:05:36,120
They really remember faces for years.

229
00:05:36,180 --> 00:05:38,340
Some even copy human words.

230
00:05:38,340 --> 00:05:40,000
But a walnut -sized brain holding

231
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:41,340
a human mind?

232
00:05:41,340 --> 00:05:42,500
Impossible.

233
00:05:42,540 --> 00:05:43,820
And how smart are crows right

234
00:05:43,820 --> 00:05:44,640
now?

235
00:05:44,720 --> 00:05:47,120
Experts. Still argue.

236
00:05:47,180 --> 00:05:48,940
Some see real reasoning about cause

237
00:05:48,940 --> 00:05:50,140
and effect.

238
00:05:50,180 --> 00:05:51,200
Others say much of it is

239
00:05:51,200 --> 00:05:52,840
fast trial and error.

240
00:05:52,840 --> 00:05:54,440
Which side are you on?

241
00:05:54,440 --> 00:05:55,380
But tonight,

242
00:05:55,380 --> 00:05:57,460
far across the city, something else

243
00:05:57,460 --> 00:05:58,980
opens its eyes.

244
00:05:59,040 --> 00:06:01,640
Quietly, patiently in the walls.

245
00:06:01,640 --> 00:06:03,480
And it has been listening to,

246
00:06:03,480 --> 00:06:04,700
what do you think?

247
00:06:04,700 --> 00:06:06,280
Tell us in the comments and

248
00:06:06,280 --> 00:06:07,420
tell us which animal we should

249
00:06:07,420 --> 00:06:08,740
make smart next.

250
00:06:08,740 --> 00:06:11,140
Hit like and subscribe for more.
